## Object of the Invention

An object of the invention is to provide an organic light-emitting diode (OLED) module capable of detecting, by electrostatic capacitance, the presence, position, movement, or gesture of an object located at or above a light-emitting surface.

Another object is to integrate a hover sensor and a light-emitting-element driver in a compact module while reducing interference between a capacitance-detection signal and a drive signal supplied to the OLED.

A further object is to improve hover-detection accuracy despite variations in OLED luminance, drive current, switching frequency, ambient electrical noise, temperature, or parasitic capacitance.

A further object is to provide an OLED module in which light-emission operation and capacitance-detection operation are coordinated in time, frequency, phase, or amplitude so that stable sensing can be performed while maintaining desired display or illumination characteristics.

A further object is to provide a thin and visually unobtrusive sensing structure by arranging at least part of a sensor electrode, wiring, shielding layer, or sensing circuit within, on, or adjacent to the OLED panel.

A further object is to enable contactless operation of a smart device or lighting device, including activation, dimming, color control, user-interface navigation, authentication assistance, or gesture input.

---

## Core Technical Configuration

An OLED module according to one aspect of the invention comprises:

1. **an OLED panel** including:
   - a first electrode;
   - a second electrode; and
   - an organic light-emitting layer disposed between the first and second electrodes;

2. **a light-emitting-element driver** configured to supply a drive voltage or drive current to the OLED panel and thereby control at least one of:
   - emission and non-emission;
   - luminance;
   - color;
   - emission timing; or
   - an emission region;

3. **an electrostatic-capacitance hover sensor** including at least one sensor electrode disposed in, on, behind, around, or adjacent to the OLED panel, the hover sensor being configured to detect a capacitance variation caused by an object located apart from or approaching a sensing surface of the OLED module;

4. **a sensing circuit** electrically coupled to the sensor electrode and configured to generate sensor data representing at least one of:
   - presence or absence of the object;
   - distance or proximity of the object;
   - a position of the object;
   - a movement direction;
   - a movement trajectory; or
   - a contactless gesture; and

5. **a controller** coupled to the light-emitting-element driver and the sensing circuit and configured to control the OLED panel based at least partly on the sensor data.

The object may include a finger, hand, stylus, body part, conductive tool, or another object having an electrostatic influence, and hover detection may be performed without physical contact with the sensing surface.

---

## Sensor and Panel Arrangement

The sensor electrode may comprise one or more self-capacitance electrodes, mutual-capacitance electrodes, transmission electrodes, reception electrodes, guard electrodes, reference electrodes, or combinations thereof.

The sensor electrode may be:

- formed on a substrate of the OLED panel;
- disposed between an OLED substrate and a cover member;
- disposed on an outer surface of an encapsulation layer;
- located behind the OLED stack relative to the light-emitting surface;
- arranged around a perimeter of a light-emitting region;
- patterned as a transparent conductive layer overlapping the light-emitting region; or
- formed using an OLED electrode, power-supply line, touch electrode, conductive frame, or other conductive member that is selectively shared between light-emission and sensing functions.

Where the sensor electrode overlaps the light-emitting region, the electrode may be formed from a transparent conductive material, a fine metal mesh, conductive nanowires, graphene, or another optically transmissive conductor.

The hover sensor may include a plurality of electrodes arranged in a one-dimensional or two-dimensional array. The sensing circuit may determine the position or movement of an object from capacitance values associated with two or more electrodes.

---

## Interference-Reduction Configuration

The controller may coordinate operation of the sensing circuit and the light-emitting-element driver to suppress electrical interference. In particular, the controller may perform one or more of the following:

- measure capacitance during a blanking interval, non-emission interval, or reduced-emission interval;
- suspend or hold OLED drive switching during a sensing interval;
- synchronize a capacitance excitation signal with an OLED scan, pulse-width-modulation, or current-control signal;
- select a sensing frequency separated from a frequency or harmonic used by the OLED driver;
- change the sensing frequency in response to detected interference;
- subtract a reference value representing OLED-drive-induced capacitance variation;
- compensate sensor data according to luminance, drive current, emission color, temperature, or an active emission region;
- drive a shield or guard electrode at a fixed potential or at a potential tracking the sensor electrode;
- obtain first sensor data during a first OLED drive state and second sensor data during a second OLED drive state and determine an object-related capacitance component from a difference between the first and second sensor data; or
- apply filtering, baseline tracking, common-mode rejection, or noise cancellation to the sensor data.

The OLED panel may continue to emit light during sensing. Alternatively, emission may be interrupted for a sensing interval shorter than a visually perceptible period.

---

## Shared-Electrode Configuration

In one embodiment, at least one electrode of the OLED panel is selectively used as a hover-sensing electrode. A switching circuit connects the shared electrode to the light-emitting-element driver during a light-emission period and to the sensing circuit during a sensing period.

The controller may alternately establish:

- a **light-emission state**, in which drive power is supplied to the OLED; and
- a **capacitance-sensing state**, in which the shared electrode receives a sensing signal or is connected to a capacitance measurement circuit.

A power electrode, common cathode, common anode, segmented OLED electrode, or electrically isolated portion thereof may serve as the shared electrode. A hold circuit or storage capacitor may maintain an emission-control state while capacitance is measured.

---

## Detection and Control Functions

The controller may compare a measured capacitance or compensated capacitance value with one or more thresholds. Different thresholds may correspond to:

- initial approach;
- near-hover;
- imminent contact;
- contact;
- withdrawal; or
- different estimated distances.

The controller may identify gestures such as approach, withdrawal, dwell, swipe, circular motion, waving, selection, or directional movement. Hysteresis or temporal qualification may be used to prevent unintended activation.

Based on the sensor data, the controller may:

- turn the OLED on or off;
- increase or decrease luminance;
- change color or color temperature;
- select an emission region;
- display an icon, menu, or notification;
- execute a device command;
- wake a device from a low-power state;
- adjust sensing sensitivity;
- communicate an input command to an external processor; or
- control another electrical load.

The OLED module may include a communication interface for transmitting sensor data or control information to a host device by a wired or wireless connection.

---

## Smart-Device Application

A smart device may comprise the OLED module, a processor, memory, and a device housing. The OLED module may provide both visual output and contactless input at a common surface.

The smart device may be, for example:

- a smartphone, tablet, wearable device, or smart watch;
- a household appliance;
- a vehicle display or control panel;
- a medical or sanitation-oriented terminal;
- an access-control device;
- an information kiosk;
- a smart mirror;
- a remote controller; or
- an Internet-of-Things device.

The processor may execute a function in response to hover sensor data. For example, the processor may display a control object when a hand approaches, enlarge or highlight a selectable item according to hover position, and execute the associated command upon detection of a dwell or further approach.

---

## Lighting-Device Application

A lighting device may comprise:

- the OLED module;
- a power-supply circuit;
- a housing or support;
- and optionally a communication circuit or environmental sensor.

The hover sensor may detect a hand or object near a light-emitting surface or designated control region. The controller may adjust the lighting state without requiring physical contact.

Controllable lighting parameters may include:

- on/off state;
- luminous intensity;
- color;
- color temperature;
- emission pattern;
- illumination direction;
- activated OLED segment; or
- transition speed.

The lighting device may be implemented as a luminaire, wall panel, desk lamp, vehicle interior light, architectural lighting panel, cabinet light, mirror light, emergency light, or decorative light.

---

## Compact Independent-Claim Style Formulation

**An organic light-emitting diode module comprising:**

- an organic light-emitting panel including an organic light-emitting layer between first and second electrodes;
- a light-emitting-element driver configured to drive the organic light-emitting panel;
- at least one electrostatic-capacitance sensor electrode arranged relative to the organic light-emitting panel so as to detect an object spaced from a sensing surface;
- a sensing circuit configured to measure a capacitance associated with the sensor electrode and generate hover sensor data; and
- a controller configured to coordinate operation of the light-emitting-element driver and the sensing circuit and to control at least one light-emission characteristic of the organic light-emitting panel based on the hover sensor data,

wherein the controller performs capacitance measurement during a sensing condition selected to reduce interference caused by operation of the light-emitting-element driver.
